{% trace-scenario title="What should the framework expose?" prompt="Two frameworks complete the same task, but one hides retries and tool decisions. Which is safer to operate?" outcome="Prefer the framework that exposes the execution boundary and lets your team inspect, test, and control consequential steps." /%}

## At a glance

This guide is for **teams choosing an AI agent framework**. It helps you **evaluate operational fit beyond a quick demo**. It does not provide a universal framework ranking or replace a proof of concept on your workload.

### The framework scorecard

Use **Control → Evidence → Tests → Operations**. A framework is useful when it reduces implementation work without making failures opaque.

## Start with the workflow, not the library

Write the task, tools, state, approval boundaries, latency target, deployment model, and failure responses first. [AI agent architecture](/blog/ai-agent-architecture) explains why the parent task and decision boundaries matter more than the number of abstractions in a framework.

| Question | Evidence to request |
| --- | --- |
| Can we control tools? | Typed arguments, permissions, approvals |
| Can we inspect runs? | Parent/child spans and attempt history |
| Can we test behavior? | Fixtures, replay, evaluator hooks |
| Can we recover? | Timeouts, cancellation, retries, rollback |
| Can we change providers? | Clear model and adapter boundary |

## Run a failure-focused proof of concept

Do not test only a successful conversation. Include a timeout, malformed tool argument, empty retrieval, duplicate event, partial state, user correction, and approval rejection. Record what the framework handles, what your code must implement, and what remains invisible.

## Keep ownership explicit

Framework defaults are not your reliability policy. Set budgets, permissions, retention, redaction, and alerting in your application. [Building production-ready AI agents](/blog/building-production-ready-ai-agents) provides a broader readiness checklist.

## Release checklist

- Define the workflow and boundaries before selecting abstractions.
- Verify tool permissions and output validation.
- Trace parent tasks, model attempts, tools, and decisions.
- Test degraded paths and cancellation.
- Document framework behavior your team still owns.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Is an AI agent framework required?" answer="No. A focused workflow can be clearer with direct model and tool calls. Use a framework when its control, testing, or orchestration benefits justify the added abstraction." /%}
{% faq-item question="How should frameworks be compared?" answer="Compare them on your representative task and failure set, including observability, tool control, latency, cost, portability, and the amount of custom operational code required." /%}
{% faq-item question="Can a framework guarantee reliable agents?" answer="No. Reliability depends on the complete system: model, tools, data, policies, runtime, tests, and operations. A framework can expose or hide those concerns but cannot remove them." /%}

